#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101652} :activity/id #uuid "145ac031-2a3a-531e-b695-a527eb78e27d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048525 :topic/id #uuid "a6b8e054-0a05-55a5-8475-3e9f607dc63b" :topic/math-academy-id 1230} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102025 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to use the graph of a function's derivative to maximize the function. Let's understand how this process works by studying a specific example.\n\nThe graph of $y = f'(x),$ the *derivative* of $f(x),$ is shown in the picture below. \n\n![](images/1e/1ef0f93de1825106880b3fdcd3c704c72e0748df277e5098064d6ee9abc9bdf8.png)\n\nThe areas of the shaded regions are indicated on the diagram. Additionally, it is known that $f(-2)=3.$ Our goal is find the *maximum* value of $f(x)$ on the interval $x\\in [-2,2].$\n\nTo find the maximum value of $f(x),$ we use the candidates test.\n\nAccording to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. Therefore, we proceed as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ so there are no non-stationary critical points. Additionally, we see that $f'(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are \n\n$$\nx=-2,\\quad x=-1,\\quad x=2.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n- The value of $f(x)$ at the left endpoint is given:\n\n$$\nf(-2) = 3\n$$\n\n- To compute $f(-1),$ we apply the fundamental theorem of calculus. Since the area of the left triangle in our picture equals ${\\color{red}{1}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To compute $f(2),$ we apply the fundamental theorem of calculus once more. Using the fact that the area of the left triangle equals ${\\color{red}{1}}$ and the area of the right triangle equals ${\\color{blue}{\\dfrac32}},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3+1-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we *subtracted* the area of the right triangle since it lies *under* the $x$-axis.\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\left\\lbrace 3, 4, \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right\\rbrace = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=-1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0533716e-c304-5c45-8aa3-f51853f120c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 819 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f2b6e44b-f469-543a-a1ac-99aecca54d2f" :step/math-academy-id 1572 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101814}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100857 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100698 :question/id #uuid "be6b45d4-9257-5a84-8e19-50a315871d3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-442"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96c23e89-1183-51f8-96e1-860b01688bd1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049388}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511565}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves"} :step/id #uuid "adca67a5-ec48-5624-857c-3dfe325add06" :step/math-academy-id 1573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100858 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100699 :question/id #uuid "9fbd4cac-e923-57fd-94f3-79